Original commit body from 8f417f29e5a5aeb465b2a7af646401eff42b7e8e:

Addresses WI-giloh. nim.py resolved every call through the flat registry,
which keeps ONE declaration per name (the last registered), with no import
scope, no same-file preference and no visibility check. A call could bind a
private proc (no `*`) of a module the caller never imported: 96 edges into
nitter's private `redis_cache.get`, including `cfg.get(...)` in config.nim,
which declares its own `get*` five lines up.

A per-run `_NimScope` index replaces that lookup for calls. Pass 1 records
each file's import / from / include / export statements; symbol registration
records every same-named declaration. A call sees, nearest first:
1. its own file;
2. the files `include` joins to it, in either direction (an include pastes
   the file in, so the group shares private declarations and imports;
   chronos's selectors2 includes one of three platform files);
3. exported declarations of the modules it imports (every file of each
   imported module's include group), narrowed by `from ... import` and
   `except`, extended transitively through `export m` (module), `export name`
   (one symbol) and `export m except x`.
A module-qualified call (`m.f()`, or through an `as` alias) looks only in
that module. When the module declares no such name, the receiver is a value
shadowing the module name (nitter: a `query` parameter and a `query`
module), so the call is treated as UFCS. A library-module qualifier
(`su.strip`) stays unresolved. Module paths resolve relative to the importer,
then by module-path suffix anywhere in the repo (the search path, e.g. a
nimble `src/`); `std/` and `pkg/` are never repo files. Several visible
candidates go through ListNameResolver, whose confidence (1/sqrt N) says the
pick is a guess; Nim chooses by argument types, which the analyzer lacks.

The re-export closure is a worklist over (module, filter) states. An earlier
draft memoised with an in-progress cycle guard, which cached a partial answer
whose content depended on evaluation order; the include handling had the
same flaw and became include groups.

Tests: test_nim_scope_resolution.py, 30 multi-file fixtures on the production
entry point. Every negative case was red on the old lookup; fixtures where
two same-named declarations compete call BOTH sides, so a one-per-name
registry fails whichever file registers last (the first draft passed four of
them by discovery-order luck). The include-group grant, the from-filter
narrowing, the shadowing fallback and export-except were each mutated back
and watched go red.

MEASURED (analyze_nim; nitter from the corpus, plus shallow clones of
nim-chronos c679d29, pixie 30bb510, nimble 33a6bc8; the corpus holds no
other Nim repo). Diffed by call site:
- call edges: unchanged in all four (30,199). Only targets move.
- cross-file edges into a private proc: 1,555 -> 42 (101/482/364/608 ->
  0/42/0/0). All 42 are inside chronos's selectors2 include group, where
  private visibility is legal.
- resolved calls 15,354 -> 13,423 (nitter 902->827, chronos 7,749->7,002,
  pixie 2,456->2,153, nimble 4,247->3,441).
  1,931 went unresolved: 1,131 had bound a private proc of another module,
  800 an exported proc of a module the caller cannot see. Reading samples of
  those 800 found two recall losses in the draft (an imported module's
  included files; a value shadowing a module name) and a parsing gap
  (`export m except x`), all fixed and pinned. A fresh sample of 20 drawn
  after the fixes: 20 correct losses (`add`, `len`, `inc`, `addLast`,
  `Option.get`, `Result.error` bound to project procs; nimble's
  `Path`-typed `dirExists` / `fileExists` / `writeFile` from paths.nim, which
  the callers do not import; compat/syntaxes' `syntaxes.parseAll`, which
  names the compiler's proc, not its own wrapper).
- retargeted 2,528: 871 to a single visible candidate (14 sampled, all
  corrections: own file over a sibling, the visible module over a private
  one); 1,657 are now an overload guess among several visible modules, one
  arbitrary pick replaced by another that lowers its confidence -- NOT
  claimed as a precision gain either way.
- nitter's named instances: redis_cache `get` 104 edges (96 cross-file) ->
  8 (0 cross-file). types.nim `add*` 73 -> 64: 63 are in files that import
  types directly and 1 is in types.nim; there seq's system `add` and types'
  `add*` are both visible and only argument types decide. That residual is receiver
  typing (INV-vigaf's linker direction), not scope.

NOT DONE: templates / macros / iterators / converters are still not symbols
(WI-losaf), so a call to a template stays unresolved and an included file's
template calls can fall through to a sibling's proc. Imports inside a
`when` branch count as visible (the analyzer cannot evaluate the condition).

